sections.s2_technical:
The first case for a Role is compute. When the statement service runs on EC2, you attach an instance profile that wraps a Role. The SDK's default credential provider automatically fetches temporary credentials from the instance metadata endpoint, uses them, and refreshes them before they expire. You write zero credential-handling code.

```java
import software.amazon.awssdk.auth.credentials.DefaultCredentialsProvider;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.s3.S3Client;

// On EC2/Lambda this picks up the Role's temporary credentials automatically.
S3Client s3 = S3Client.builder()
        .credentialsProvider(DefaultCredentialsProvider.create())
        .build();
```

The second case is cross-account access. National Bank of India has a production account and an analytics account. The analytics account must read production's settlement data. Instead of copying production access keys into the analytics account, you create a Role in production whose trust policy allows the analytics account to assume it. The analytics job calls `assumeRole` and gets temporary production credentials scoped to exactly what it needs.

The third case is federation — users logging in through an identity provider or Single Sign-On assume a Role and never get a permanent AWS User at all.

Why never embed long-lived keys: an access key is a two-part secret (key ID and secret key) that does not expire on its own. If it lands in a Git commit, a Docker image layer, a log line, or a stack trace, it is compromised forever until someone notices and rotates it. Attackers scan public repositories for exactly these strings within seconds of a push. Temporary credentials from STS cut the blast radius to the token's lifetime, usually one hour.

A senior nuance: even for local development you should use `aws sso login` or a named profile rather than keys in the code. And when a Role calls another Role, the role chaining caps the session to one hour regardless of the configured maximum.

sections.s4d_java:
```java
// ─── File: RoleDemo.java ───
package com.bank.role;

import java.time.*;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class RoleDemo {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger("Role");
    static {
        Handler h = new ConsoleHandler();
        h.setFormatter(new BankLogFormatter());
        log.setUseParentHandlers(false);
        log.addHandler(h);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AssumeRoleService sts = new AssumeRoleService();

        // RIGHT WAY: EC2 assumes a Role → temporary credentials.
        TemporaryCredentials creds = sts.assumeRole(
                "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/SettlementReaderRole",
                "ec2-settlement-session", Duration.ofHours(1));
        log.info(() -> "Assumed Role, token expires at " + creds.expiry());

        SettlementReader reader = new SettlementReader(creds);
        log.info(() -> "Read settlement file: " + reader.read("SETL-2026-10-29.csv"));

        // Cross-account: analytics assumes a production Role.
        TemporaryCredentials xacct = sts.assumeRole(
                "arn:aws:iam::999988887777:role/ProdSettlementReadRole",
                "analytics-session", Duration.ofMinutes(30));
        log.info(() -> "Cross-account token expires at " + xacct.expiry());

        // WRONG WAY illustrated — never do this:
        log.warning("Embedding an access key in code (AKIA...) would never expire and leak forever");
    }
}

// ─── Would be in TemporaryCredentials.java ───
class TemporaryCredentials {
    private final String accessKeyId, secretKey, sessionToken;
    private final Instant expiry;
    TemporaryCredentials(String id, String secret, String token, Instant expiry) {
        this.accessKeyId = id; this.secretKey = secret; this.sessionToken = token; this.expiry = expiry;
    }
    Instant expiry() { return expiry; }
    boolean expired() { return Instant.now().isAfter(expiry); }
    String sessionToken() { return sessionToken; }
}

// ─── Would be in AssumeRoleService.java ───
class AssumeRoleService {
    // Simulates STS AssumeRole: issues short-lived credentials.
    TemporaryCredentials assumeRole(String roleArn, String sessionName, Duration ttl) {
        String token = "STS-" + sessionName + "-" + System.nanoTime();
        return new TemporaryCredentials("ASIA-temp", "temp-secret", token, Instant.now().plus(ttl));
    }
}

// ─── Would be in SettlementReader.java ───
class SettlementReader {
    private final TemporaryCredentials creds;
    SettlementReader(TemporaryCredentials creds) { this.creds = creds; }
    String read(String key) {
        if (creds.expired()) throw new IllegalStateException("Credentials expired — refresh via STS");
        return "contents-of-" + key;  // would be an S3 GetObject call
    }
}

// ─── Would be in BankLogFormatter.java ───
class BankLogFormatter extends Formatter {
    @Override public String format(LogRecord r) {
        return String.format("%1$tF %1$tT [%2$s] %3$s%n", r.getMillis(), r.getLevel(), r.getMessage());
    }
}
